<?nvs
// The coherent tier over TLS, at a database index that is not zero: the same
// two members as `examples/cache.nvs`, against a store
// `examples/cache-shared-tls.toml` reaches encrypted and switches before the
// first command.
//
// [ADR 0142](/docs/decisions/0142.md) is why neither
// shows up here: the grant is `cache.shared`, which names the *store* and asks
// no address, so a store reached over TLS at index seven is not a special case
// of it. Nothing below mentions the transport or the index, and that is the
// claim — a program written against the shared tier is already written against
// all three spellings of it.
//
// What the second line below can see, and what it cannot: `greeting` is
// `examples/cache.nvs`'s entry, written with no lifetime at index zero of this
// same Redis, and reading it *before* this program writes anything is how a
// connection says which database it is on. A client that never sent `SELECT`
// would answer that read with `hello` and then agree with itself about every
// write after it, which is why the read comes first and why the key is one this
// fixture never writes. That the two steps also go out *in order*, and again on
// the socket the store drops, is
// `auth_and_select_precede_the_first_command_in_that_order` and
// `a_reconnect_after_a_dropped_socket_sends_both_again` in
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/cache/redis.rs`, which can see the wire itself.
//
// The two lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

var $shared = Core\Cache::shared();

// Index zero's entry, asked for before anything here has written a key.
var $atZero = $shared->get('greeting');

// Out of the request heap and back into it, over a socket nothing on the way
// can read. The value is compared rather than the absence, because "the store
// answered" is the weaker half of what a round trip proves. The key is a
// constant for `examples/cache-shared-socket.nvs`'s reason: the shared store
// does not forget when this process exits, so a drawn key would leave one entry
// per run behind at an index nothing else sweeps.
$shared->put('tls-greeting', 'hello');
var $seen = $shared->get('tls-greeting');
if ($seen == 'hello') {
    echo 'put and get agree over TLS', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'the store did not answer with what it was given', "\n";
}

// The entry above is on a connection that was switched away from zero before it
// carried anything: this one read zero's own key and did not find it.
if ($seen == 'hello' && $atZero == null) {
    echo 'the entry is at the configured index and not at zero', "\n";
} else {
    echo 'the connection answered out of index zero', "\n";
}
